{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., where a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes, typically the zero or low-payoff outcomes). To assess skewness in this context, we examine how the probability mass is distributed between favorable (non-zero) and unfavorable (zero) outcomes. Lottery A has a 0.2% chance of winning 3000 Euros and a 99.8% chance of winning nothing. Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of winning 6000 Euros and a 99.9% chance of winning nothing. Thus, Lottery A assigns slightly less probability to the unfavorable outcome (99.8% vs. 99.9%) and more to the favorable one. In terms of skewness, both lotteries are highly right-skewed, but Lottery B is slightly more right-skewed because it has a higher probability of yielding nothing (99.9% > 99.8%) despite offering a higher potential gain. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery B, as it is more right-skewed.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports having looked at both lotteries A and B. They note that the expected value (referred to as 'theoretischer Gewinn') is the same for both, which aligns with the fact that both lotteries have an expected value of 6 Euros (3000 × 0.002 = 6; 6000 × 0.001 = 6). The individual states that the decisive factor was that the probability of getting nothing (‘komplett leer auszugehen’) is lower in Lottery A (99.8%) compared to Lottery B (99.9%). Therefore, the individual focused on the probability of the unfavorable outcome (zero payoff) and preferred the option with the lower chance of receiving nothing. Their justification centers on minimizing the chance of a zero outcome, not on skewness per se, but on a component that partially overlaps with skewness (the weight on unfavorable outcomes).",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness and specifically prefers the more right-skewed lottery, which is Lottery B (higher probability of zero outcome). However, the individual explicitly prefers Lottery A because it has a lower chance of yielding nothing, which is the opposite of what the reason would dictate. While both the reason and the individual consider the probability of unfavorable outcomes, the individual uses this information to avoid right-skewness (preferring less probability on zero), whereas the decision reason favors greater right-skewness. Thus, although the same type of information (probability of zero outcome) is considered, the rule applied by the individual contradicts the rule of the decision reason. The individual uses a reasoning pattern that is conceptually related but directionally opposite to the given decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically favoring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). In this context, 'unfavorable' outcomes are more negative (larger losses). Lottery A has an 80% chance of -4000 and 20% chance of 0; thus, the majority of its probability mass (80%) is on the worse outcome (-4000), making it right-skewed. Lottery B is certain at -3000, so it has no variance and thus no skewness in the usual sense (skewness = 0). However, since all probability is on a single intermediate loss, it is less right-skewed than A, which concentrates more probability on a worse outcome. Therefore, according to the reason, Lottery A is more right-skewed than B, and the reason would prefer A. The information needed to apply the reason (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual reports reviewing both lotteries and deciding on B. The justification given is that in B, they would lose only 3000 Euros, whereas in A, they would 'rechnerisch' (mathematically or on average) lose 3200 Euros. This indicates the individual considered the expected value of Lottery A: 0.8 * (-4000) + 0.2 * 0 = -3200. The individual did not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or the idea of concentrating losses on worse outcomes. Instead, the deliberation focused on comparing a certain loss (B) to the expected loss in A, using expected value as a criterion. The justification is based on minimizing expected loss, not on preferring more right-skewed distributions.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness and prefers more right-skewed lotteries (favoring A), but the individual’s reasoning is based on expected value (favoring B due to lower expected loss). The information considered by the individual (expected monetary value) differs from the information required by the decision reason (distribution shape and skewness). Furthermore, the individual’s justification (minimizing expected loss) contradicts the reason’s rule (preferring more right-skewed options). There is no mention or implication of distributional asymmetry or skewness in the verbal report. Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
